+**Interfaces:**
+- Consumes: `parse_file(path) -> (list[Reading], list[int])` and `FileError` (Tasks 1–2);
+  `Store(path)` with `marker(name) -> Marker | None`, `set_marker(name, state, failures=0, detail="")`,
+  `add(key, reading: dict) -> bool`, `commit()`, `rollback()`, `close()`; `Marker(state, failures, detail)`.
+- Produces, for Task 4:
+  - `INBOX_DIR = "inbox"`, `POISON_AFTER = 3`
+  - `@dataclass class Summary` with int fields, in this order: `files, imported, skipped, failed,
+    poisoned, stored, duplicates, rejected`, all defaulting to 0.
+  - `key(reading) -> str`
+  - `exports(inbox) -> list[str]`: names of the `*.csv` regular files in `inbox`, sorted.
+  - `run(inbox, store, report=lambda line: None) -> Summary`. `report` is called with one line,
+    `"<name>: <state> (<failures>): <detail>"`, for each file that failed or was poisoned this run.
+    Any exception other than `FileError` rolls back and propagates.
+
+The `report` keyword is this plan's addition to the spec's `run(inbox, store)`: the stderr line of
+§6.3 needs per-file detail that `Summary` does not carry, and a callback keeps `run` free of
+`sys.stderr` so the tests can collect the lines. `run(inbox, store)` still works as the spec writes it.
